<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baiyue</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hundred Yue</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baiyue, or Hundred Yue, were the various non-Chinese peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in the first millennium BCE, living in states such as Minyue, Nanyue and Ouyue. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered much of their land in the late 3rd century BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> absorbed their kingdoms by 110 BCE, gradually assimilating them into Chinese culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baiyue.jpg" alt="Baiyue" width="320" height="611" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Discott · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yue_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
